<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      Material 3 Expressive app theme (Settings > Theme), built from the
      guidance at https://m3.material.io/blog/building-with-m3-expressive.

      Unlike Theme.Bada (MaterialComponents *Bridge*, so plain widgets stay
      AppCompat), this theme is a full Theme.Material3Expressive descendant:
      `<Button>` inflates as an M3 Expressive MaterialButton (round shape
      that morphs on press and selection), CircularProgressIndicator is the
      wavy variant, and ExpressiveViewInflater swaps SwitchCompat tags for
      MaterialSwitch. Every badaXxx token from attrs_theme.xml is mapped to
      an M3 role here; AppThemeTokensTest fails the build if one is missing.

      How each M3 Expressive style maps into Bada:
        * Color: the generated Vibrant 2025 scheme in colors_m3e.xml, seeded
          from the brand blue. Page = surface, navigation bar = surface
          container (the M3 default pairing), cards and list items =
          surface container (overridden from the list default, surface, so
          they separate from the page), fields = surface container highest,
          two steps above their container ("more than one step apart").
        * Shape: the corner radius scale (m3e_corner_* dimens). Information-
          dense cards use large (16dp), not extra large ("Be careful not to
          apply large or full corners to information-dense components");
          segmented list items use large outer / extra small inner corners;
          sheets keep extra large (28dp). Fields inside a 20dp-padded card get
          extra small (4dp), since outer radius minus padding is below zero.
          Buttons stay fully round against the squarer cards for tension.
        * Icons: Material Symbols Rounded for the navigation and overflow
          icons; the selected Settings tab uses the filled symbol.
        * Type: the 30-style M3 scale re-pointed at Google Sans Flex, which
          the shape guidance pairs with the M3 shapes. Emphasized styles are
          kept for headlines and the primary actions.
        * Motion: MDC-Android ships the spring tokens but does not wire them
          into components, so ExpressiveMotion applies them to Bada's own
          animations (sheet entrance, toggles, tab icons).

      The window variants below repeat each Theme.Bada.* window setup so
      AppThemes can swap a manifest theme for its Expressive twin before
      super.onCreate().
    -->
